  "content": {
    "id": "p38-q2-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "Let $f:[0,1]\\to[0,3]$ be defined by $f(x)=3x$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p38-q2-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $f$ is injective.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "If $f(x)=f(y)$, then $3x=3y$, so $x=y$. Hence $f$ is injective.",
          "worked": "Let $x,y\\in[0,1]$ and suppose $f(x)=f(y)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}3x&=3y\\\\x&=y\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $f$ is injective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p38-q2-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that $f$ is surjective.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For every $y\\in[0,3]$, the input $x=\\frac{y}{3}\\in[0,1]$ satisfies $f(x)=y$. Hence $f$ is surjective.",
          "worked": "Let $y\\in[0,3]$. Choose $x=\\dfrac{y}{3}$. Since $0\\le y\\le3$, we have $0\\le x\\le1$, so $x$ is in the domain.\n$$\\begin{align*}f\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)&=3\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)\\\\f\\left(\\frac{y}{3}\\right)&=y\\end{align*}$$\nEvery element of $[0,3]$ has a preimage, so $f$ is surjective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p38-q2-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Confirm that $[0,1]$ and $[0,3]$ have the same cardinality.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$|[0,1]|=|[0,3]|$, since $f$ is a bijection.",
          "worked": "Parts (a) and (b) show that $f$ is injective and surjective. Thus $f:[0,1]\\to[0,3]$ is a bijection, establishing a one-to-one correspondence between the intervals. Therefore $|[0,1]|=|[0,3]|$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      }
    ]
  },
